\tag{fdip}
\title{Transfer, KMS states and superselection}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{How do time evolution and symmetry appear in the language of observables and
states? The first section shows that the crossing block of a positive
definite precision sets the rank of the linear reflection Gram, and that a
finite Gibbs state obeys the conjugation identity. The Gaussian and gauge
transfer operators remain the positive operators already constructed for
those systems.}

\p{The following section asks which observables remain when a symmetry is
imposed.}

